	<subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>

	<conjecture xml:id="rep_voltage-diagrams">
		<title>Voltage Diagrams</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>A <term>voltage diagram</term> shows how the voltage changes throughout a circuit.</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>A vertical arrow represents the voltage across a circuit element.</alert>  The length of the arrow represents the size of the change, and the direction of the arrow represents whether the voltage increases or decreases.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The diagram starts at some reference point.</alert>  A common reference is the bottom left corner, often at the negative terminal of a battery.  You may typically choose this voltage to be zero.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The voltages around a complete loop sum to zero.</alert>  The voltage diagram should end at the same reference point as it began, and the voltage at this final point should be the same as the initial voltage, according to <xref ref="loop-rule"/>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<alert>The voltage diagram shows one complete loop.</alert>  If a circuit has multiple loops, it may need multiple voltage diagrams!
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		<figure xml:id="fig-voltage_diagram">
		<title>Voltage Diagram Example</title>
		<caption>A basic circuit and its associated voltage diagram. Two points, A and B, are defined between circuit elements. Voltage increases moving clockwise from point A to point B, then decreases by the same amount from B to A.</caption>
		<image source="circ/voltage-diagram.png" width = "100%" >
		<description>Left: a battery and a circuit element connected by wires labeled A and B.  Right: A graph of voltage, with 0 at point A, +Vbat at point B.</description></image>
		</figure>
		</statement>
	</conjecture>
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

